{"title":"Creating your first Quarto document","markdown":{"headingText":"Creating your first Quarto document","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n## Setting up your Quarto project\n\n1. Create a new directory for your project.\n2. Launch VSCode\n3. Open the command palette (Ctrl-shift-P) and begin typing ``` Quarto: Create Project```\n4. Select \"Book Project\"\n5. Choose the Project directory you created in step 1.\n\nThis creates:\n\n- a simple `_quarto.yml` file (effectively your premable)\n- a few chapters (`*.qmd`)\n- a `references.bib` (a simple bibtex file)\n\n\n::: {.callout-tip}\n\nYou may find it useful to create a `figures` and `chapters` folder to keep your top level directory tidy.\n\n:::\n\n::: {.callout-note}\n\nIn this demo we use \"Book Project\" as the starting point. Alternatively, you may choose a \"Default Project\" (which has fewer default files) or any of the other templates. \n\nYou can visit the repo for this book to see the final structure of the project: [Getting Started With Quarto](https://github.com/joshualim/Getting-Started-With-Quarto)\n\n:::\n\n\n\n\n## Rendering your project\n\n### Rendering from VSCode\nYou can render your project by:\n\n 1. Open a .qmd file (e.g. `index.qmd`) in VSCode.\n 2. Open the command palette (Ctrl-shift-P) and begin typing ``` Quarto: Render Project```\n 3. Select what to render: ```All formats | HTML | PDF ```\n\n::: {.callout-tip}\n\nAlternatively, you can launch a live preview which automatically updates as you edit your content. \nLaunch ```Quarto: Preview``` or ```Ctrl-Shift-K```\n\n:::\n\n\n### Rendering from the command line\n\nYou can either use the Terminal in VSCode (or just have a terminal open) and render the document from a terminal using:\n\n```bash\nquarto render\n```\n\n::: {.callout-tip}\n\nAlternatively, you can launch a live preview which automatically updates as you edit your content.\n\n```bash\nquarto preview\n```\n\n:::\n\n### Quick preview\nFor a quick preview of simple markdown code, the ```Markdown All in One``` VSCode extension, will show an instant preview of any single markdown document. You can run this Preview from the VSCode command palette of ```Ctrl-Shift-V```\n\n\n::: {.callout-warning}\n\nPlease note the quick preview is just a simple markdown display preview (it won't know about references, figures, code) and doesn't rebuild your whole project, but can be useful for quick previews to check things look *roughly* right.\n\n:::\n\n\n::: {.callout-note}\n\nCongratulations, you've built your first Quarto project! In the following chapters we will learn how to add content.\n\n:::\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":false,"code-overflow":"wrap","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","